1
00:00:02,195 --> 00:00:05,527
Modern JavaScript gives us an alternative

2
00:00:05,639 --> 00:00:09,020
promise handling syntax which we can also

3
00:00:09,068 --> 00:00:11,199
use instead of then and catch,

4
00:00:12,289 --> 00:00:14,211
and that's the async/await syntax.

5
00:00:15,012 --> 00:00:17,095
It's also used by Deno, which is why I wanna

6
00:00:17,095 --> 00:00:17,768
show it to you here,

7
00:00:18,297 --> 00:00:20,893
and it's just syntactic sugar for promises.

8
00:00:20,941 --> 00:00:21,950
That's really important.

9
00:00:23,184 --> 00:00:25,811
Whenever you see async/await, and you'll see

10
00:00:25,811 --> 00:00:27,157
it in action in just a second,

11
00:00:27,734 --> 00:00:28,407
under the hood,

12
00:00:29,113 --> 00:00:31,764
We have a promise with then and catch. We

13
00:00:31,764 --> 00:00:34,398
just don't write that then and catch block

14
00:00:34,559 --> 00:00:36,904
on our own. Instead, we use async await

15
00:00:36,904 --> 00:00:39,234
because, as you will see, it's a bit easier

16
00:00:39,298 --> 00:00:41,932
to write. It's nicer code, easier to read.

17
00:00:43,009 --> 00:00:45,980
It makes asynchronous code, promise-based

18
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:48,551
code, look like synchronous code. It looks

19
00:00:48,551 --> 00:00:48,936
like

20
00:00:49,498 --> 00:00:51,603
we don't have those functions which we pass

21
00:00:51,603 --> 00:00:54,061
to then. It looks like we just have lines of

22
00:00:54,093 --> 00:00:56,886
code that execute after each other as we're

23
00:00:56,902 --> 00:00:57,271
used to.

24
00:00:58,249 --> 00:01:00,204
And in order to use async/await, we

25
00:01:00,268 --> 00:01:04,194
typically require a function marked as async

26
00:01:04,194 --> 00:01:06,838
with the async keyword, and then we can use

27
00:01:06,838 --> 00:01:10,011
the await keyword inside of that function to

28
00:01:10,107 --> 00:01:12,735
await for a promise to resolve.

29
00:01:13,328 --> 00:01:16,277
I'm saying typically here because with Deno,

30
00:01:16,757 --> 00:01:17,927
but also with modern

31
00:01:18,777 --> 00:01:20,027
Node.js actually,

32
00:01:20,684 --> 00:01:23,251
you also can use top-level await. You can

33
00:01:23,251 --> 00:01:25,596
use the await keyword outside of async

34
00:01:25,596 --> 00:01:26,126
functions.

35
00:01:26,801 --> 00:01:28,536
But we're already diving into this a little

36
00:01:28,568 --> 00:01:29,387
bit too deeply.

37
00:01:29,917 --> 00:01:32,696
Let's first of all see what async await is

38
00:01:32,824 --> 00:01:35,298
really all about and how the syntax looks

39
00:01:35,298 --> 00:01:35,555
like.

40
00:01:36,342 --> 00:01:39,619
So back here in this code, we got this fetch

41
00:01:39,731 --> 00:01:42,189
function here. We got this fetch function

42
00:01:42,285 --> 00:01:44,358
which then gives us this promise chain.

43
00:01:45,096 --> 00:01:47,859
Now it is important to recognize that if we

44
00:01:47,907 --> 00:01:51,582
log hello world here after the fetch

45
00:01:51,694 --> 00:01:52,255
request,

46
00:01:52,896 --> 00:01:54,883
you will actually see the result from that

47
00:01:54,899 --> 00:01:59,305
log before you see that or this log message.

48
00:02:00,171 --> 00:02:00,956
If we reload,

49
00:02:01,773 --> 00:02:04,129
we see hello world first before we see the

50
00:02:04,129 --> 00:02:06,612
status code or the extracted data.

51
00:02:07,237 --> 00:02:10,041
And that's not by accident or because of a

52
00:02:10,041 --> 00:02:12,926
bug, but that happens because JavaScript

53
00:02:13,006 --> 00:02:14,496
execution doesn't pause.

54
00:02:15,233 --> 00:02:16,499
It executes this line

55
00:02:17,076 --> 00:02:20,187
and registers all those then handlers, you

56
00:02:20,203 --> 00:02:20,572
could say,

57
00:02:21,326 --> 00:02:23,138
but then it moves on right away to the next

58
00:02:23,186 --> 00:02:25,222
line and to the next line and to the next

59
00:02:25,254 --> 00:02:25,463
line.

60
00:02:26,297 --> 00:02:29,247
And those handlers are only registered to

61
00:02:29,359 --> 00:02:32,486
run at some point in the future once this

62
00:02:32,486 --> 00:02:34,491
promise resolved. That's why we see this

63
00:02:34,491 --> 00:02:34,827
first.

64
00:02:36,094 --> 00:02:38,067
Now that is regular JavaScript.

65
00:02:38,981 --> 00:02:41,594
Now using these then methods and passing

66
00:02:41,626 --> 00:02:44,433
those callback functions to then can be a

67
00:02:44,497 --> 00:02:47,901
bit cumbersome and you might not like this

68
00:02:48,029 --> 00:02:48,575
syntax.

69
00:02:49,795 --> 00:02:51,930
For that, you can use async await. And as I

70
00:02:51,994 --> 00:02:55,156
said, you need a function marked as

71
00:02:55,333 --> 00:02:58,046
asynchronous to use the await keyword in it,

72
00:02:58,463 --> 00:03:01,048
at least at the moment in JavaScript. In

73
00:03:01,208 --> 00:03:01,690
Deno,

74
00:03:02,316 --> 00:03:04,483
which is also JavaScript but with extra

75
00:03:04,483 --> 00:03:07,517
features, you will not need this extra async

76
00:03:07,533 --> 00:03:09,684
function wrapper, but I'll come back to that

77
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:11,209
once we write some Deno code.

78
00:03:11,949 --> 00:03:14,509
So here I'll add a function sendRequest

79
00:03:15,668 --> 00:03:18,018
which simply holds all that fetch logic.

80
00:03:19,612 --> 00:03:21,045
And then here I just call sendRequest.

81
00:03:22,422 --> 00:03:24,414
And now we can actually add the async

82
00:03:24,414 --> 00:03:26,326
keyword in front of that function

83
00:03:26,921 --> 00:03:28,318
to tell JavaScript

84
00:03:28,849 --> 00:03:31,146
that here we wanna use the await keyword

85
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:32,435
inside of that function.

86
00:03:33,261 --> 00:03:35,965
Async functions by default return a promise.

87
00:03:36,030 --> 00:03:38,227
That's important. Even if you don't have a

88
00:03:38,227 --> 00:03:40,122
return statement in there, they always

89
00:03:40,219 --> 00:03:42,789
return an empty promise then, but still a

90
00:03:42,789 --> 00:03:43,271
promise.

91
00:03:43,914 --> 00:03:46,581
And even more importantly for us here, we

92
00:03:46,581 --> 00:03:47,995
can use this await keyword.

93
00:03:48,589 --> 00:03:49,602
And how do we use it?

94
00:03:50,437 --> 00:03:52,622
Well, instead of getting the response in

95
00:03:52,686 --> 00:03:52,959
this

96
00:03:53,731 --> 00:03:54,807
argument function here,

97
00:03:55,739 --> 00:03:57,345
it would be nice if we could type

98
00:03:57,988 --> 00:04:00,623
response equals fetch. So the result of

99
00:04:00,623 --> 00:04:02,551
fetch, that's what we're used to in

100
00:04:02,551 --> 00:04:04,959
JavaScript, right? We call a function, it

101
00:04:04,976 --> 00:04:07,110
returns a result, and we can store that

102
00:04:07,142 --> 00:04:09,116
result in a constant or variable.

103
00:04:10,111 --> 00:04:11,989
Now here that doesn't work because that

104
00:04:12,182 --> 00:04:15,183
promise is an object which just eventually

105
00:04:15,327 --> 00:04:16,884
gives us the final result.

106
00:04:17,510 --> 00:04:20,062
So when I store fetch in response like this,

107
00:04:20,206 --> 00:04:23,015
I store the promise in response and not the

108
00:04:23,015 --> 00:04:25,374
result the promise yields in the end.

109
00:04:26,321 --> 00:04:27,894
We can change this with await.

110
00:04:28,487 --> 00:04:31,194
By adding await in front of a promise,

111
00:04:31,792 --> 00:04:33,700
and that's important, you can only use it in

112
00:04:33,733 --> 00:04:34,687
front of a promise.

113
00:04:35,511 --> 00:04:37,872
You wait for that promise to resolve

114
00:04:38,648 --> 00:04:40,627
and you store the resolved value

115
00:04:41,179 --> 00:04:42,446
in that constant then.

116
00:04:43,779 --> 00:04:46,135
And now this replaces this then block.

117
00:04:47,575 --> 00:04:49,096
We don't need it anymore. We got the

118
00:04:49,096 --> 00:04:50,390
response stored here.

119
00:04:50,972 --> 00:04:53,754
We can then console.log response.status here

120
00:04:54,838 --> 00:04:56,164
and we can get our

121
00:04:56,712 --> 00:04:58,730
data, our extracted data,

122
00:04:59,618 --> 00:05:03,607
by awaiting response.json. We await again

123
00:05:03,736 --> 00:05:06,259
because JSON also returns

124
00:05:06,837 --> 00:05:08,275
a promise, as you can see here.

125
00:05:10,457 --> 00:05:12,316
And then we can get rid of this as well and

126
00:05:12,332 --> 00:05:14,354
just console.log the extracted data.

127
00:05:15,551 --> 00:05:17,443
And I'll talk about catch in a second.

128
00:05:18,957 --> 00:05:21,586
So now this replaces the code we had before.

129
00:05:22,212 --> 00:05:24,138
And it looks totally different, but under

130
00:05:24,138 --> 00:05:27,348
the hood, this code is transformed back into

131
00:05:27,428 --> 00:05:29,001
that then chaining.

132
00:05:29,739 --> 00:05:31,488
It's really important to keep in mind that

133
00:05:31,584 --> 00:05:32,788
this invisibly

134
00:05:33,671 --> 00:05:35,789
is kind of transformed back into this

135
00:05:35,853 --> 00:05:38,309
function that was passed to then. This is

136
00:05:38,405 --> 00:05:40,588
just more readable and looks more like

137
00:05:40,668 --> 00:05:43,348
regular JavaScript code we're used to, but

138
00:05:43,380 --> 00:05:45,595
it's super important to be aware that it's

139
00:05:45,627 --> 00:05:48,852
just a behind-the-scenes transformation that

140
00:05:48,852 --> 00:05:51,129
JavaScript doesn't magically change here.

141
00:05:51,257 --> 00:05:52,235
That's really important.

142
00:05:53,486 --> 00:05:55,538
Now to handle errors, you can then use

143
00:05:55,666 --> 00:05:56,596
try-catch

144
00:05:57,269 --> 00:05:59,931
and wrap the code that could fail with try

145
00:06:00,797 --> 00:06:02,432
to then catch any error here

146
00:06:03,121 --> 00:06:05,494
and execute your error handling code

147
00:06:05,526 --> 00:06:06,023
thereafter.

148
00:06:06,889 --> 00:06:09,037
So that's how you would do it for normal

149
00:06:09,069 --> 00:06:11,683
synchronous code and you can also use this

150
00:06:11,683 --> 00:06:12,629
on async/await.

151
00:06:13,462 --> 00:06:15,370
And with that, if we save that and reload in

152
00:06:15,370 --> 00:06:17,839
a modern browser, async/await is supported

153
00:06:18,176 --> 00:06:20,341
And we therefore see exactly the same result

154
00:06:20,341 --> 00:06:20,998
as before,

155
00:06:21,624 --> 00:06:23,708
but this now is a bit more readable.

156
00:06:24,382 --> 00:06:27,573
So async/await is optional. Promises are a

157
00:06:27,637 --> 00:06:29,593
core feature built into JavaScript.

158
00:06:30,042 --> 00:06:32,367
Async/await is just syntactic sugar,

159
00:06:33,009 --> 00:06:35,687
also built into JavaScript, but not a brand

160
00:06:35,751 --> 00:06:39,616
new feature. Instead, it's just a new syntax

161
00:06:39,760 --> 00:06:41,251
for working with promises,

162
00:06:41,925 --> 00:06:43,817
and it's important because we will use it in

163
00:06:43,849 --> 00:06:44,298
this course.
